jalan sendiri dan sesama. Bukan untuk menjadi pelita di ruang tertutup yang hanya dinikmati segelintir orang saja."</p><p>Hening menyelimuti ruangan. Hanya terdengar detak jam dinding — tak lelah, tak memihak — terus berjalan seakan tak peduli siapa menang dan siapa kalah. Aku merasakan dadaku sesak, seakan ada beban yang baru saja diletakkan di pundakku — berat, namun menumbuhkan kekuatan yang tak kuketahui sebelumnya. Cahaya matahari sore itu masuk ke dalam ruangan, menyinari lantai, meja, wajah Nyai, dan akhirnya jatuh tepat di hadapanku — terang, namun tak lagi hangat seperti yang kukira sebelumnya.</p><p>Saat itulah aku mengerti: bumi ini memang milik semua manusia. Tetapi belum semua manusia bebas memilikinya. Dan itulah — kebenaran yang mulai terkuak — perjuangan sesungguhnya baru saja dimulai.</p><p>(Diadaptasi dari novel <i>Bumi </i><i>Manusia</i> karya Pramoedya Ananta Toer)</p>', 'Pesan utama yang ingin disampaikan melalui keseluruhan teks adalah…', NULL, '["Ilmu pengetahuan yang dipelajari dari bangsa lain sering kali menyesatkan karena dibuat untuk kepentingan penguasa, sehingga seseorang harus kembali ke nilai-nilai bumi agar dapat hidup adil dan sejahtera","Perbedaan darah dan keturunan menentukan bagaimana seseorang diperlakukan oleh hukum dan masyarakat, sehingga seseorang harus memahami asal-usulnya agar dapat membela hak-haknya sendiri","Bumi dan isinya memang milik semua manusia secara adil dan merata, namun perbedaan kemampuan dan kebijaksanaan membuat belum semua orang mampu menikmati haknya tersebut","Perjuangan untuk memperoleh keadilan harus dimulai dengan menentang segala bentuk hukum yang dibuat oleh penguasa, karena hukum sejati adalah hukum yang tumbuh dari kesadaran bersama","Keadilan dan kesetaraan adalah hak sejati setiap manusia; namun ketidakadilan yang diciptakan oleh kekuasaan membuat hak tersebut belum terwujud sepenuhnya, sehingga perjuangan untuk mewujudkannya adalah tugas yang harus dipikul bersama"]', 'E', NULL, ('2026-09-01 08:42:12'::timestamp AT TIME ZONE 'UTC'), NULL),
  (426, 6, 'LBING', 1, 'PG', 'C3', '<p>The passage argues that men and women are assigned different roles in society, with women placed in a subordinate position. However, the author maintains that this position does not make women’s duties or influence less important. Instead, women are expected to exercise their influence through peaceful, loving, and benevolent means rather than through public debate, coercion, or physical force. According to the author, a woman’s greatest influence should come from her character, intelligence, kindness, humility, and ability to earn the respect and affection of those around her. This influence is primarily expected to operate within the domestic and social sphere, where women can positively affect their fathers, husbands, and sons.</p><p>The passage further argues that women may cooperate with other women in activities such as religious work, charity, motherhood, and domestic responsibilities. However, the author believes that women should not participate in political conflicts or become “combatants” in public movements. According to this view, joining a political party or using coercive influence would cause women to move beyond what the author considers their appropriate social sphere. Therefore, the author concludes that women should not be organized as participants in the abolitionist movement because political activism is presented as inconsistent with the role assigned to women.</p><p>The passage argues that the struggle against slavery has helped the author develop a deeper understanding of human rights, including women’s rights. The author considers the Anti-Slavery movement an important moral school because it teaches that all human beings possess rights based on their moral nature. Since men and women share the same moral nature, the author argues that they must essentially possess the same rights. These rights cannot be permanently taken away from a person, even when they are denied by society. Therefore, the author rejects the idea that biological sex should determine whether one person has greater rights or responsibilities than another.</p><p>The author further explains that moral duties should not be determined by sex but by the different circumstances, relationships, abilities, and responsibilities that people have in life. The central principle is that whatever is morally right for a man to do should also be morally right for a woman to do. In this way, the passage challenges traditional assumptions about gender roles and argues for a broader understanding of equality. Rather than treating women as naturally limited by their sex, the author emphasizes their shared moral nature with men as the foundation for equal rights and responsibilities.</p>', 'What is the most appropriate description of the author''s tone in Passage 2?', NULL, '["Sarcastic and dismissive","Emotional and humorous","Reasoned and strongly assertive","Uncertain and apologetic","Indifferent and detached"]', 'D', NULL, ('2026-09-01 09:49:42'::timestamp AT TIME ZONE 'UTC'), NULL),
  (427, 6, 'LBING', 2, 'PG', 'C3', '<p>The passage argues that men and women are assigned different roles in society, with women placed in a subordinate position. However, the author maintains that this position does not make women’s duties or influence less important. Instead, women are expected to exercise their influence through peaceful, loving, and benevolent means rather than through public debate, coercion, or physical force. According to the author, a woman’s greatest influence should come from her character, intelligence, kindness, humility, and ability to earn the respect and affection of those around her. This influence is primarily expected to operate within the domestic and social sphere, where women can positively affect their fathers, husbands, and sons.</p><p>The passage further argues that women may cooperate with other women in activities such as religious work, charity, motherhood, and domestic responsibilities. However, the author believes that women should not participate in political conflicts or become “combatants” in public movements. According to this view, joining a political party or using coercive influence would cause women to move beyond what the author considers their appropriate social sphere. Therefore, the author concludes that women should not be organized as participants in the abolitionist movement because political activism is presented as inconsistent with the role assigned to women.</p><p>The passage argues that the struggle against slavery has helped the author develop a deeper understanding of human rights, including women’s rights. The author considers the Anti-Slavery movement an important moral school because it teaches that all human beings possess rights based on their moral nature. Since men and women share the same moral nature, the author argues that they must essentially possess the same rights. These rights cannot be permanently taken away from a person, even when they are denied by society. Therefore, the author rejects the idea that biological sex should determine whether one person has greater rights or responsibilities than another.</p><p>The author further explains that moral duties should not be determined by sex but by the different circumstances, relationships, abilities, and responsibilities that people have in life. The central principle is that whatever is morally right for a man to do should also be morally right for a woman to do. In this way, the passage challenges traditional assumptions about gender roles and argues for a broader understanding of equality. Rather than treating women as naturally limited by their sex, the author emphasizes their shared moral nature with men as the foundation for equal rights and responsibilities.</p>', 'According to Passage 1, which of the following is NOT presented as a way for women to exercise their influence?', NULL, '["Developing their intellect and judgment","Demonstrating benevolence through their actions","Gaining respect through humility and refinement","Using physical force to achieve their goals","Influencing family members through love and respect"]', 'C', NULL, ('2026-09-01 09:49:42'::timestamp AT TIME ZONE 'UTC'), NULL),
  (428, 6, 'LBING', 3, 'PG', 'C3', '<p>The passage argues that men and women are assigned different roles in society, with women placed in a subordinate position. However, the author maintains that this position does not make women’s duties or influence less important. Instead, women are expected to exercise their influence through peaceful, loving, and benevolent means rather than through public debate, coercion, or physical force. According to the author, a woman’s greatest influence should come from her character, intelligence, kindness, humility, and ability to earn the respect and affection of those around her. This influence is primarily expected to operate within the domestic and social sphere, where women can positively affect their fathers, husbands, and sons.</p><p>The passage further argues that women may cooperate with other women in activities such as religious work, charity, motherhood, and domestic responsibilities. However, the author believes that women should not participate in political conflicts or become “combatants” in public movements. According to this view, joining a political party or using coercive influence would cause women to move beyond what the author considers their appropriate social sphere. Therefore, the author concludes that women should not be organized as participants in the abolitionist movement because political activism is presented as inconsistent with the role assigned to women.</p><p>The passage argues that the struggle against slavery has helped the author develop a deeper understanding of human rights, including women’s rights. The author considers the Anti-Slavery movement an important moral school because it teaches that all human beings possess rights based on their moral nature. Since men and women share the same moral nature, the author argues that they must essentially possess the same rights. These rights cannot be permanently taken away from a person, even when they are denied by society. Therefore, the author rejects the idea that biological sex should determine whether one person has greater rights or responsibilities than another.</p><p>The author further explains that moral duties should not be determined by sex but by the different circumstances, relationships, abilities, and responsibilities that people have in life. The central principle is that whatever is morally right for a man to do should also be morally right for a woman to do. In this way, the passage challenges traditional assumptions about gender roles and argues for a broader understanding of equality. Rather than treating women as naturally limited by their sex, the author emphasizes their shared moral nature with men as the foundation for equal rights and responsibilities.</p>', 'Which of the following best paraphrases the statement from Passage 2, “whatever it is morally right for man to do, it is morally right for woman to do”?', NULL, '["Moral standards should be applied equally to men and women, even though their social circumstances may differ.","Men and women should be expected to perform identical duties because their moral capacities are fundamentally the same.","Women should be granted the same responsibilities as men whenever they are willing to adopt traditionally male roles.","Differences in social roles are unnecessary because men and women possess identical abilities, experiences, and responsibilities.","Women''s moral decisions should be evaluated according to the responsibilities that society traditionally assigns to their sex."]', 'A', NULL, ('2026-09-01 09:49:42'::timestamp AT TIME ZONE 'UTC'), NULL),
