ejects the idea that biological sex should determine whether one person has greater rights or responsibilities than another.</p><p>The author further explains that moral duties should not be determined by sex but by the different circumstances, relationships, abilities, and responsibilities that people have in life. The central principle is that whatever is morally right for a man to do should also be morally right for a woman to do. In this way, the passage challenges traditional assumptions about gender roles and argues for a broader understanding of equality. Rather than treating women as naturally limited by their sex, the author emphasizes their shared moral nature with men as the foundation for equal rights and responsibilities.</p>', 'In Passage 1, the phrase “whatever binds her in a party conflict” refers to something that...', NULL, '["places women in organized disputes where they are expected to defend a particular side or interest","encourages women to cooperate with one another in fulfilling their religious and charitable responsibilities","allows women to influence public opinion through the personal respect they earn within their communities","strengthens women''s ability to challenge social expectations through their intellectual and moral development","enables women to influence society through kindness and love"]', 'A', NULL, ('2026-09-01 09:49:42'::timestamp AT TIME ZONE 'UTC'), NULL),
  (430, 6, 'LBING', 5, 'PG', 'C3', '<p>The passage argues that men and women are assigned different roles in society, with women placed in a subordinate position. However, the author maintains that this position does not make women’s duties or influence less important. Instead, women are expected to exercise their influence through peaceful, loving, and benevolent means rather than through public debate, coercion, or physical force. According to the author, a woman’s greatest influence should come from her character, intelligence, kindness, humility, and ability to earn the respect and affection of those around her. This influence is primarily expected to operate within the domestic and social sphere, where women can positively affect their fathers, husbands, and sons.</p><p>The passage further argues that women may cooperate with other women in activities such as religious work, charity, motherhood, and domestic responsibilities. However, the author believes that women should not participate in political conflicts or become “combatants” in public movements. According to this view, joining a political party or using coercive influence would cause women to move beyond what the author considers their appropriate social sphere. Therefore, the author concludes that women should not be organized as participants in the abolitionist movement because political activism is presented as inconsistent with the role assigned to women.</p><p>The passage argues that the struggle against slavery has helped the author develop a deeper understanding of human rights, including women’s rights. The author considers the Anti-Slavery movement an important moral school because it teaches that all human beings possess rights based on their moral nature. Since men and women share the same moral nature, the author argues that they must essentially possess the same rights. These rights cannot be permanently taken away from a person, even when they are denied by society. Therefore, the author rejects the idea that biological sex should determine whether one person has greater rights or responsibilities than another.</p><p>The author further explains that moral duties should not be determined by sex but by the different circumstances, relationships, abilities, and responsibilities that people have in life. The central principle is that whatever is morally right for a man to do should also be morally right for a woman to do. In this way, the passage challenges traditional assumptions about gender roles and argues for a broader understanding of equality. Rather than treating women as naturally limited by their sex, the author emphasizes their shared moral nature with men as the foundation for equal rights and responsibilities.</p>', 'How does the argument in Passage 2 relate to the position presented in Passage 1?', NULL, '["Passage 2 shifts the discussion from women''s social influence to slavery without addressing the assumptions about gender presented in Passage 1.","Passage 2 extends the argument in Passage 1 by showing that women''s influence can become more effective when exercised through political and social confrontation.","Passage 2 qualifies the argument in Passage 1 by suggesting that women may enter political conflicts when their domestic responsibilities are sufficiently fulfilled.","Passage 2 supports the argument in Passage 1 by presenting women''s moral nature as fundamentally different from that of men.","Passage 2 challenges the premise underlying Passage 1 by suggesting that moral rights should not be determined by sex, even when social roles may differ."]', 'E', NULL, ('2026-09-01 09:49:42'::timestamp AT TIME ZONE 'UTC'), NULL),
  (431, 6, 'LBING', 6, 'PG', 'C3', '<p>The passage argues that men and women are assigned different roles in society, with women placed in a subordinate position. However, the author maintains that this position does not make women’s duties or influence less important. Instead, women are expected to exercise their influence through peaceful, loving, and benevolent means rather than through public debate, coercion, or physical force. According to the author, a woman’s greatest influence should come from her character, intelligence, kindness, humility, and ability to earn the respect and affection of those around her. This influence is primarily expected to operate within the domestic and social sphere, where women can positively affect their fathers, husbands, and sons.</p><p>The passage further argues that women may cooperate with other women in activities such as religious work, charity, motherhood, and domestic responsibilities. However, the author believes that women should not participate in political conflicts or become “combatants” in public movements. According to this view, joining a political party or using coercive influence would cause women to move beyond what the author considers their appropriate social sphere. Therefore, the author concludes that women should not be organized as participants in the abolitionist movement because political activism is presented as inconsistent with the role assigned to women.</p><p>The passage argues that the struggle against slavery has helped the author develop a deeper understanding of human rights, including women’s rights. The author considers the Anti-Slavery movement an important moral school because it teaches that all human beings possess rights based on their moral nature. Since men and women share the same moral nature, the author argues that they must essentially possess the same rights. These rights cannot be permanently taken away from a person, even when they are denied by society. Therefore, the author rejects the idea that biological sex should determine whether one person has greater rights or responsibilities than another.</p><p>The author further explains that moral duties should not be determined by sex but by the different circumstances, relationships, abilities, and responsibilities that people have in life. The central principle is that whatever is morally right for a man to do should also be morally right for a woman to do. In this way, the passage challenges traditional assumptions about gender roles and argues for a broader understanding of equality. Rather than treating women as naturally limited by their sex, the author emphasizes their shared moral nature with men as the foundation for equal rights and responsibilities.</p>', 'According to Passage 1, which of the following best describes the kind of influence the author considers appropriate for women?', NULL, '["Influence gained through intellectual competition and participation in public debate","Influence exercised through respect, affection, benevolence, and personal character","Influence achieved by organizing political groups and shaping public sentiment","Influence established through direct participation in conflicts involving social reform","Influence strengthened by using authority to persuade others to accept women''s demands"]', 'B', NULL, ('2026-09-01 09:49:42'::timestamp AT TIME ZONE 'UTC'), NULL),
  (432, 6, 'LBING', 7, 'PG', 'C3', '<p>The passage argues that men and women are assigned different roles in society, with women placed in a subordinate position. However, the author maintains that this position does not make women’s duties or influence less important. Instead, women are expected to exercise their influence through peaceful, loving, and benevolent means rather than through public debate, coercion, or physical force. According to the author, a woman’s greatest influence should come from her character, intelligence, kindness, humility, and ability to earn the respect and affection of those around her. This influence is primarily expected to operate within the domestic and social sphere, where women can positively affect their fathers, husbands, and sons.</p><p>The passage further argues that women may cooperate with other women in activities such as religious work, charity, motherhood, and domestic responsibilities. However, the author believes that women should not participate in political conflicts or become “combatants” in public movements. According to this view, joining a political party or using coercive influence would cause women to move beyond what the author considers their appropriate social sphere. Therefore, the author concludes that women should not be organized as participants in the abolitionist movement because political activism is presented as inconsistent with the role assigned to women.</p><p>The passage argues that the struggle against slavery has helped the author develop a deeper understanding of human rights, including women’s rights. The author considers the Anti-Slavery movement an important moral school because it teaches that all human beings possess rights based on their moral nature. Since men and women share the same moral nature, the author argues that they must essentially possess the same rights. These rights cannot be permanently taken away from a person, even when they are denied by society. Therefore, the author rejects the idea that biological sex should determine whether one person has greater rights or responsibilities than another.</p><p>The author further explains that moral duties should not be determined by sex but by the different circumstances, relationships, abilities, and responsibilities that people have in life. The central principle is that whatever is morally right for a man to do should also be morally right for a woman to do. In this way, the passage challenges traditional assumptions about gender roles and argues for a broader understanding of equality. Rather than treating women as naturally limited by their sex, the author emphasizes their shared moral nature with men as the foundation for equal rights and responsibilities.</p>', 'In Passage 1, Beecher implies that women’s effect on public life is largely', NULL, '["overlooked, because few men are interested in women’s thoughts about politics.","symbolic, because women tend to be more idealistic about politics than men are.","unnecessary, because men are able to govern society themselves.","indirect, because women exert their influence within the home and family life.","Persuasively, by encouraging men to reconsider social issues through public political arguments."]', 'D', NULL, ('2026-09-01 09:49:42'::timestamp AT TIME ZONE 'UTC'), NULL),
  (433, 6, 'LBING', 8, 'PG', 'C3', '<p>The passage argues that men and women are assigned different roles in society, with women placed in a subordinate position. However, the author maintains that this position does not make women’s duties or influence less important. Instead, women are expected to exercise their influence through peaceful, loving, and benevolent means rather than through public debate, coercion, or physical force. According to the author, a woman’s greatest influence should come from her character, intelligence, kindness, humility, and ability to earn the respect and affection of those around her. This influence is primarily expected to operate within the domestic and social sphere, where women can positively affect their fathers, husbands, and sons.</p><p>The passage further argues that women may cooperate with other women in activities such as religious work, charity, motherhood, and domestic responsibilities. However, the author believes that women should not participate in political conflicts or become “combatants” in public movements. According to this view, joining a political party or using coercive influence would cause women to move beyond what the author considers their appropriate social sphere. Therefore, the author concludes that women should not be organized as participants in the abolitionist movement because political activism is presented as inconsistent with the role assigned to women.</p><p>The passage argues that the struggle against slavery has helped the author develop a deeper understanding of human rights, including women’s rights. The author considers the Anti-Slavery movement an important moral school because it teaches that all human beings possess rights based on their moral nature. Since men and women share the same moral nature, the author argues that they must essentially possess the same rights. These rights cannot be permanently taken away from a person, even when they are denied by society. Therefore, the author rejects the idea that biological sex should determine whether one person has greater rights or responsibilities than another.</p><p>The author further explains that moral duties should not be determined by sex but by the different circumstances, relationships, abilities, and responsibilities that people have in life. The central principle is that whatever is morally right for a man to do should also be morally right for a woman to do. In this way, the passage challenges traditional assumptions about gender roles and argues for a broader understanding of equality. Rather than treating women as naturally limited by their sex, the author emphasizes their shared moral nature with men as the foundation for equal rights and responsibilities.</p>', 'Based on the passages, both authors would agree with which of the following claims?', NULL, '["Women have moral duties and responsibilities.","Men often work selflessly for political change.","The ethical obligations of women are often undervalued.","Women can influence society effectively when they fulfill the roles traditionally expected of them.","Political activism is as important for women as it is for men."]', 'A', NULL, ('2026-09-01 09:49:42'::timestamp AT TIME ZONE 'UTC'), NULL),
  (434, 6, 'LBING', 9, 'PG', 'C3', '<p>When it comes to energy, efficiency is generally considered beneficial because it reduces waste, saves resources, and can help both the economy and the environment. However, the idea of efficiency becomes more complicated when applied to food production. Modern conventional agriculture has become highly efficient at producing large amounts of food from limited farmland through fertilizers, mechanization, and irrigation.</p><p>Despite its efficiency, conventional agriculture has serious environmental costs. It requires large amounts of energy, synthetic fertilizers, and chemical pesticides. Although these methods increase crop yields, excess nitrogen from fertilizers can run into oceans and create polluted dead zones. These environmental problems have led many people, especially environmentalists, to support organic farming as a more sustainable alternative.</p><p>However, a major analysis published in Nature challenges the assumption that organic farming is always better for the environment. Researchers compared 66 studies involving 34 crop species and found that organic farming produced lower yields for every crop type. The difference was relatively small for some legumes and fruit crops, but conventional farming produced more than 25% higher yields for major crops such as corn, wheat, and many vegetables.</p><p>One major reason for the difference is nitrogen. Conventional agriculture uses large amounts of synthetic fertilizer, which provides plants with nitrogen more quickly than the compost and cover crops used in organic farming. This contributes to higher yields but also creates environmental problems because much of the nitrogen does not remain in the crops. Conventional agriculture also relies heavily on pesticides, which can have harmful unintended effects.</p><p>Therefore, neither conventional nor organic agriculture can be considered completely ideal. Conventional farming is currently more effective for producing large amounts of staple calories, while organic methods may be more suitable for certain fruits and vegetables. The study suggests that the best future agricultural system may combine the strengths of both approaches and apply different methods according to geographic, economic, and social conditions.</p>', 'According to the passage, a significant attribute of conventional agriculture is its ability to', '/soal/to-4sep2026/2bdbca645d8d57fc.png', '["produce awide variety of fruits and vegetables.","lessen the necessity of nitrogen in plant growth.","satisfy the dietary needs of the world’s population.","maximize the output of cultivated land.","reduce the environmental risks associated with intensive farming."]', 'D', NULL, ('2026-09-01 09:49:42'::timestamp AT TIME ZONE 'UTC'), NULL),
  (435, 6, 'LBING', 10, 'PG', 'C3', '<p>When it comes to energy, efficiency is generally considered beneficial because it reduces waste, saves resources, and can help both the economy and the environment. However, the idea of efficiency becomes more complicated when applied to food production. Modern conventional agriculture has become highly efficient at producing large amounts of food from limited farmland through fertilizers, mechanization, and irrigation.</p><p>Despite its efficiency, conventional agriculture has serious environmental costs. It requires large amounts of energy, synthetic fertilizers, and chemical pesticides. Although these methods increase crop yields, excess nitrogen from fertilizers can run into oceans and create polluted dead zones. These environmental problems have led many people, especially environmentalists, to support organic farming as a more sustainable alternative.</p><p>However, a major analysis published in Nature challenges the assumption that organic farming is always better for the environment. Researchers compared 66 studies involving 34 crop species and found that organic farming produced lower yields for every crop type. The difference was relatively small for some legumes and fruit crops, but conventional farming produced more than 25% higher yields for major crops such as corn, wheat, and many vegetables.</p><p>One major reason for the difference is nitrogen. Conventional agriculture uses large amounts of synthetic fertilizer, which provides plants with nitrogen more quickly than the compost and cover crops used in organic farming. This contributes to higher yields but also creates environmental problems because much of the nitrogen does not remain in the crops. Conventional agriculture also relies heavily on pesticides, which can have harmful unintended effects.</p><p>Therefore, neither conventional nor organic agriculture can be considered completely ideal. Conventional farming is currently more effective for producing large amounts of staple calories, while organic methods may be more suitable for certain fruits and vegetables. The study suggests that the best future agricultural system may combine the strengths of both approaches and apply different methods according to geographic, economic, and social conditions.</p>', 'Which statement best expresses a relationship between organic farming and conventional farming that is presented in the passage?', '/soal/to-4sep2026/2bdbca645d8d57fc.png', '["Both are equally sustainable, but they differ dramatically in the amount of land they require to produce equivalent yields.","Both rely on artificial chemicals for pest control, but organic farmers use the chemicals sparingly in conjunction with natural remedies.","Both use nitrogen to encourage plant growth, but the nitrogen used in conventional farming comes from synthetic sources.","Both create a substantial amount of nitrogen runoff, but only the type of nitrogen found in fertilizers used in conventional farming can be dangerous.","Both can contribute to global food production, but they differ in their effectiveness depending on the type of crop being cultivated."]', 'C', NULL, ('2026-09-01 09:49:42'::timestamp AT TIME ZONE 'UTC'), NULL),
  (436, 6, 'LBING', 11, 'PG', 'C3', '<p>When it comes to energy, efficiency is generally considered beneficial because it reduces waste, saves resources, and can help both the economy and the environment. However, the idea of efficiency becomes more complicated when applied to food production. Modern conventional agriculture has become highly efficient at producing large amounts of food from limited farmland through fertilizers, mechanization, and irrigation.</p><p>Despite its efficiency, conventional agriculture has serious environmental costs. It requires large amounts of energy, synthetic fertilizers, and chemical pesticides. Although these methods increase crop yields, excess nitrogen from fertilizers can run into oceans and create polluted dead zones. These environmental problems have led many people, especially environmentalists, to support organic farming as a more sustainable alternative.</p><p>However, a major analysis published in Nature challenges the assumption that organic farming is always better for the environment. Researchers compared 66 studies involving 34 crop species and found that organic farming produced lower yields for every crop type. The difference was relatively small for some legumes and fruit crops, but conventional farming produced more than 25% higher yields for major crops such as corn, wheat, and many vegetables.</p><p>One major reason for the difference is nitrogen. Conventional agriculture uses large amounts of synthetic fertilizer, which provides plants with nitrogen more quickly than the compost and cover crops used in organic farming. This contributes to higher yields but also creates environmental problems because much of the nitrogen does not remain in the crops. Conventional agriculture also relies heavily on pesticides, which can have harmful unintended effects.</p><p>Therefore, neither conventional nor organic agriculture can be considered completely ideal. Conventional farming is currently more effective for producing large amounts of staple calories, while organic methods may be more suitable for certain fruits and vegetables. The study suggests that the best future agricultural system may combine the strengths of both approaches and apply different methods according to geographic, economic, and social conditions.</p>', 'Which statement is best supported by the information provided in Figure 1?', '/soal/to-4sep2026/2bdbca645d8d57fc.png', '["The organic yield as a percentage of conventional yield is greater for vegetables than for fruits.","The organic yield as a percentage of conventional yield is similar for cereals and all crops.","The reported number of observations for each crop type exceeds 82.","The organic yield as a percentage of conventional yield is greater for vegetable crops than it is for oilseed crops.","The number of observations reported for oilseed crops is greater than that reported for cereal crops."]', 'B', NULL, ('2026-09-01 09:49:42'::timestamp AT TIME ZONE 'UTC'), NULL),
  (437, 6, 'LBING', 12, 'PG', 'C3', '<p>When it comes to energy, efficiency is generally considered beneficial because it reduces waste, saves resources, and can help both the economy and the environment. However, the idea of efficiency becomes more complicated when applied to food production. Modern conventional agriculture has become highly efficient at producing large amounts of food from limited farmland through fertilizers, mechanization, and irrigation.</p><p>Despite its efficiency, conventional agriculture has serious environmental costs. It requires large amounts of energy, synthetic fertilizers, and chemical pesticides. Although these methods increase crop yields, excess nitrogen from fertilizers can run into oceans and create polluted dead zones. These environmental problems have led many people, especially environmentalists, to support organic farming as a more sustainable alternative.</p><p>However, a major analysis published in Nature challenges the assumption that organic farming is always better for the environment. Researchers compared 66 studies involving 34 crop species and found that organic farming produced lower yields for every crop type. The difference was relatively small for some legumes and fruit crops, but conventional farming produced more than 25% higher yields for major crops such as corn, wheat, and many vegetables.</p><p>One major reason for the difference is nitrogen. Conventional agriculture uses large amounts of synthetic fertilizer, which provides plants with nitrogen more quickly than the compost and cover crops used in organic farming. This contributes to higher yields but also creates environmental problems because much of the nitrogen does not remain in the crops. Conventional agriculture also relies heavily on pesticides, which can have harmful unintended effects.</p><p>Therefore, neither conventional nor organic agriculture can be considered completely ideal. Conventional farming is currently more effective for producing large amounts of staple calories, while organic methods may be more suitable for certain fruits and vegetables. The study suggests that the best future agricultural system may combine the strengths of both approaches and apply different methods according to geographic, economic, and social conditions.</p>', 'Which of the following claims is supported by Figure 2?', '/soal/to-4sep2026/2bdbca645d8d57fc.png', '["Of the organically grown species represented, soybeans have a yield comparable to that of wheat.","The organically grown maize and barley represented are comparable in their yields to conventionally grown maize and barley.","Of the organically grown species represented, tomatoes have the highest yield.","The organically grown species represented have lower yields than their conventionally grown counterparts do.","The yield of organically grown wheat is substantially higher than that of organically grown soybeans."]', 'D', NULL, ('2026-09-01 09:49:42'::timestamp AT TIME ZONE 'UTC'), NULL),
  (438, 6, 'LBING', 13, 'PG', 'C3', '<p>When it comes to energy, efficiency is generally considered beneficial because it reduces waste, saves resources, and can help both the economy and the environment. However, the idea of efficiency becomes more complicated when applied to food production. Modern conventional agriculture has become highly efficient at producing large amounts of food from limited farmland through fertilizers, mechanization, and irrigation.</p><p>Despite its efficiency, conventional agriculture has serious environmental costs. It requires large amounts of energy, synthetic fertilizers, and chemical pesticides. Although these methods increase crop yields, excess nitrogen from fertilizers can run into oceans and create polluted dead zones. These environmental problems have led many people, especially environmentalists, to support organic farming as a more sustainable alternative.</p><p>However, a major analysis published in Nature challenges the assumption that organic farming is always better for the environment. Researchers compared 66 studies involving 34 crop species and found that organic farming produced lower yields for every crop type. The difference was relatively small for some legumes and fruit crops, but conventional farming produced more than 25% higher yields for major crops such as corn, wheat, and many vegetables.</p><p>One major reason for the difference is nitrogen. Conventional agriculture uses large amounts of synthetic fertilizer, which provides plants with nitrogen more quickly than the compost and cover crops used in organic farming. This contributes to higher yields but also creates environmental problems because much of the nitrogen does not remain in the crops. Conventional agriculture also relies heavily on pesticides, which can have harmful unintended effects.</p><p>Therefore, neither conventional nor organic agriculture can be considered completely ideal. Conventional farming is currently more effective for producing large amounts of staple calories, while organic methods may be more suitable for certain fruits and vegetables. The study suggests that the best future agricultural system may combine the strengths of both approaches and apply different methods according to geographic, economic, and social conditions.</p>', 'Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?', '/soal/to-4sep2026/2bdbca645d8d57fc.png', '["Conventional agriculture is more beneficial than organic farming because its higher yields outweigh its environmental costs.","Organic agriculture offers a more sustainable alternative to conventional farming because it avoids synthetic fertilizers and chemical pesticides.","The environmental value of agricultural systems depends on how effectively they balance productivity with the ecological costs associated with production.","Modern agriculture should gradually replace conventional farming with organic methods to reduce fertilizer use and environmental pollution.","The growing demand for food requires farmers to prioritize high-yield agricultural methods over concerns about environmental sustainability."]', 'C', NULL, ('2026-09-01 09:49:42'::timestamp AT TIME ZONE 'UTC'), NULL),
  (439, 6, 'LBING', 14, 'PG', 'C3', '<p>When it comes to energy, efficiency is generally considered beneficial because it reduces waste, saves resources, and can help both the economy and the environment. However, the idea of efficiency becomes more complicated when applied to food production. Modern conventional agriculture has become highly efficient at producing large amounts of food from limited farmland through fertilizers, mechanization, and irrigation.</p><p>Despite its efficiency, conventional agriculture has serious environmental costs. It requires large amounts of energy, synthetic fertilizers, and chemical pesticides. Although these methods increase crop yields, excess nitrogen from fertilizers can run into oceans and create polluted dead zones. These environmental problems have led many people, especially environmentalists, to support organic farming as a more sustainable alternative.</p><p>However, a major analysis published in Nature challenges the assumption that organic farming is always better for the environment. Researchers compared 66 studies involving 34 crop species and found that organic farming produced lower yields for every crop type. The difference was relatively small for some legumes and fruit crops, but conventional farming produced more than 25% higher yields for major crops such as corn, wheat, and many vegetables.</p><p>One major reason for the difference is nitrogen. Conventional agriculture uses large amounts of synthetic fertilizer, which provides plants with nitrogen more quickly than the compost and cover crops used in organic farming. This contributes to higher yields but also creates environmental problems because much of the nitrogen does not remain in the crops. Conventional agriculture also relies heavily on pesticides, which can have harmful unintended effects.</p><p>Therefore, neither conventional nor organic agriculture can be considered completely ideal. Conventional farming is currently more effective for producing large amounts of staple calories, while organic methods may be more suitable for certain fruits and vegetables. The study suggests that the best future agricultural system may combine the strengths of both approaches and apply different methods according to geographic, economic, and social conditions.</p>', 'Which of the following best describes the organization of the passage?', '/soal/to-4sep2026/2bdbca645d8d57fc.png', '["It introduces the environmental advantages of efficiency, challenges the assumption that organic farming is more sustainable, compares the two systems, and proposes a balanced approach.","It describes the development of conventional agriculture, explains the benefits of organic farming, presents evidence supporting organic yields, and recommends expanding organic production.","It introduces organic farming as an environmental solution, examines its limitations in producing staple crops, and concludes that conventional agriculture should replace it.","It compares the economic performance of conventional and organic farming, evaluates their production costs, and proposes different systems according to regional markets.","It presents the environmental problems caused by conventional farming, identifies organic farming as their primary solution, and explains why farmers should adopt organic practices."]', 'A', NULL, ('2026-09-01 09:49:42'::timestamp AT TIME ZONE 'UTC'), NULL),
  (440, 6, 'LBING', 15, 'PG', 'C3', '<p>When it comes to energy, efficiency is generally considered beneficial because it reduces waste, saves resources, and can help both the economy and the environment. However, the idea of efficiency becomes more complicated when applied to food production. Modern conventional agriculture has become highly efficient at producing large amounts of food from limited farmland through fertilizers, mechanization, and irrigation.</p><p>Despite its efficiency, conventional agriculture has serious environmental costs. It requires large amounts of energy, synthetic fertilizers, and chemical pesticides. Although these methods increase crop yields, excess nitrogen from fertilizers can run into oceans and create polluted dead zones. These environmental problems have led many people, especially environmentalists, to support organic farming as a more sustainable alternative.</p><p>However, a major analysis published in Nature challenges the assumption that organic farming is always better for the environment. Researchers compared 66 studies involving 34 crop species and found that organic farming produced lower yields for every crop type. The difference was relatively small for some legumes and fruit crops, but conventional farming produced more than 25% higher yields for major crops such as corn, wheat, and many vegetables.</p><p>One major reason for the difference is nitrogen. Conventional agriculture uses large amounts of synthetic fertilizer, which provides plants with nitrogen more quickly than the compost and cover crops used in organic farming. This contributes to higher yields but also creates environmental problems because much of the nitrogen does not remain in the crops. Conventional agriculture also relies heavily on pesticides, which can have harmful unintended effects.</p><p>Therefore, neither conventional nor organic agriculture can be considered completely ideal. Conventional farming is currently more effective for producing large amounts of staple calories, while organic methods may be more suitable for certain fruits and vegetables. The study suggests that the best future agricultural system may combine the strengths of both approaches and apply different methods according to geographic, economic, and social conditions.</p>', 'Which of the following conclusions can most reasonably be drawn from the passage?', '/soal/to-4sep2026/2bdbca645d8d57fc.png', '["Increasing agricultural productivity will inevitably result in greater environmental damage because higher yields require more intensive farming practices.","The environmental benefits of organic farming may be limited when its lower productivity requires substantially more land to produce the same amount of food.","Conventional agriculture will eventually become more sustainable as farmers develop more efficient fertilizers and reduce their dependence on chemical pesticides.","Organic farming is most appropriate for staple crops because these crops require fewer synthetic inputs and can be produced efficiently on smaller areas of land.","The most sustainable agricultural system would be one that completely eliminates conventional practices while maintaining the productivity of modern farming. @MayaPutri"]', 'B', NULL, ('2026-09-01 09:49:42'::timestamp AT TIME ZONE 'UTC'), NULL),
  (441, 6, 'LBING', 16, 'PG', 'C3', '<p>I’ve been wondering whether sunscreen is actually as harmless as people say. Some products contain chemical filters and other ingredients that can irritate sensitive skin. There are also concerns online about certain sunscreen ingredients being absorbed into the body. If people are using sunscreen every day, shouldn’t we be more careful about what we put on our skin? Maybe relying on shade, clothing, and avoiding intense sunlight would be a safer approach than using sunscreen regularly.</p><p>@RakaWijaya</p><p>I understand the concern, but I think the discussion often leaves out an important point. Sunscreen is designed to reduce exposure to ultraviolet radiation, which can damage skin. The fact that some ingredients may interact with the skin or be absorbed does not automatically mean they are harmful. What matters is the evidence about actual health risks and how the product is used.</p><p>@NadiaSari</p><p>I agree that we shouldn’t assume every sunscreen is dangerous. However, I think people should still pay attention to the products they choose. Someone with sensitive skin may react differently to certain ingredients. Sunscreen also shouldn’t become an excuse for staying in the sun for long periods. Protective clothing and shade can still be useful, especially when sunlight is intense.</p><p>@DimasArif</p><p>I’m not convinced that sunscreen is necessary for everyone every day. If someone spends most of the day indoors and has little sun exposure, applying sunscreen repeatedly may seem unnecessary. People should consider their circumstances instead of treating one skincare habit as a universal rule.</p><p>@SintaRahma</p><p>The problem with the “sunscreen is dangerous” argument is that it can make people focus on hypothetical risks while ignoring established ones. Sun exposure itself can cause significant damage to skin. That doesn’t mean every sunscreen product is perfect, but rejecting sunscreen altogether because of concerns about particular ingredients seems like an overreaction. A better approach is to consider the evidence, choose appropriate protection, and avoid excessive sun exposure.</p>', 'Which of the following best captures the central issue discussed in the thread?', NULL, '["Whether sunscreen products should be completely replaced by protective clothing and shade.","Whether concerns about sunscreen ingredients justify reconsidering the way people protect themselves from sunlight.","Whether people who spend most of their time indoors need to follow the same skincare routines as outdoor workers.","Whether chemical ingredients in skincare products are generally more dangerous than environmental exposure.","Whether sunscreen companies should provide consumers with more information about the ingredients in their products."]', 'B', NULL, ('2026-09-01 09:49:42'::timestamp AT TIME ZONE 'UTC'), NULL),
  (442, 6, 'LBING', 17, 'PG', 'C3', '<p>I’ve been wondering whether sunscreen is actually as harmless as people say. Some products contain chemical filters and other ingredients that can irritate sensitive skin. There are also concerns online about certain sunscreen ingredients being absorbed into the body. If people are using sunscreen every day, shouldn’t we be more careful about what we put on our skin? Maybe relying on shade, clothing, and avoiding intense sunlight would be a safer approach than using sunscreen regularly.</p><p>@RakaWijaya</p><p>I understand the concern, but I think the discussion often leaves out an important point. Sunscreen is designed to reduce exposure to ultraviolet radiation, which can damage skin. The fact that some ingredients may interact with the skin or be absorbed does not automatically mean they are harmful. What matters is the evidence about actual health risks and how the product is used.</p><p>@NadiaSari</p><p>I agree that we shouldn’t assume every sunscreen is dangerous. However, I think people should still pay attention to the products they choose. Someone with sensitive skin may react differently to certain ingredients. Sunscreen also shouldn’t become an excuse for staying in the sun for long periods. Protective clothing and shade can still be useful, especially when sunlight is intense.</p><p>@DimasArif</p><p>I’m not convinced that sunscreen is necessary for everyone every day. If someone spends most of the day indoors and has little sun exposure, applying sunscreen repeatedly may seem unnecessary. People should consider their circumstances instead of treating one skincare habit as a universal rule.</p><p>@SintaRahma</p><p>The problem with the “sunscreen is dangerous” argument is that it can make people focus on hypothetical risks while ignoring established ones. Sun exposure itself can cause significant damage to skin. That doesn’t mean every sunscreen product is perfect, but rejecting sunscreen altogether because of concerns about particular ingredients seems like an overreaction. A better approach is to consider the evidence, choose appropriate protection, and avoid excessive sun exposure.</p>', 'How does Sinta’s response relate to Maya’s original argument?', NULL, '["She supports Maya’s conclusion that people should avoid sunscreen because its ingredients may enter the body.","She rejects Maya’s concerns entirely by claiming that all sunscreen products have been proven completely safe.","She extends Maya’s argument by suggesting that protective clothing is more effective than sunscreen in every situation.","She shifts the discussion toward indoor sun exposure rather than addressing the potential risks of sunscreen ingredients.","She accepts Maya’s concerns but argues that the risks of sun exposure should also be considered when evaluating sunscreen."]', 'E', NULL, ('2026-09-01 09:49:42'::timestamp AT TIME ZONE 'UTC'), NULL),
  (443, 6, 'LBING', 18, 'PG', 'C3', '<p>I’ve been wondering whether sunscreen is actually as harmless as people say. Some products contain chemical filters and other ingredients that can irritate sensitive skin. There are also concerns online about certain sunscreen ingredients being absorbed into the body. If people are using sunscreen every day, shouldn’t we be more careful about what we put on our skin? Maybe relying on shade, clothing, and avoiding intense sunlight would be a safer approach than using sunscreen regularly.</p><p>@RakaWijaya</p><p>I understand the concern, but I think the discussion often leaves out an important point. Sunscreen is designed to reduce exposure to ultraviolet radiation, which can damage skin. The fact that some ingredients may interact with the skin or be absorbed does not automatically mean they are harmful. What matters is the evidence about actual health risks and how the product is used.</p><p>@NadiaSari</p><p>I agree that we shouldn’t assume every sunscreen is dangerous. However, I think people should still pay attention to the products they choose. Someone with sensitive skin may react differently to certain ingredients. Sunscreen also shouldn’t become an excuse for staying in the sun for long periods. Protective clothing and shade can still be useful, especially when sunlight is intense.</p><p>@DimasArif</p><p>I’m not convinced that sunscreen is necessary for everyone every day. If someone spends most of the day indoors and has little sun exposure, applying sunscreen repeatedly may seem unnecessary. People should consider their circumstances instead of treating one skincare habit as a universal rule.</p><p>@SintaRahma</p><p>The problem with the “sunscreen is dangerous” argument is that it can make people focus on hypothetical risks while ignoring established ones. Sun exposure itself can cause significant damage to skin. That doesn’t mean every sunscreen product is perfect, but rejecting sunscreen altogether because of concerns about particular ingredients seems like an overreaction. A better approach is to consider the evidence, choose appropriate protection, and avoid excessive sun exposure.</p>', 'Which conclusion can most reasonably be drawn from the discussion among the four respondents?', NULL, '["The safest approach is to avoid both sunscreen and direct sunlight whenever possible.","Individual reactions to sunscreen are possible, but this does not by itself establish that sunscreen is generally unsafe.","People who have sensitive skin should avoid all forms of sunscreen and depend entirely on clothing.","The benefits of sunscreen are limited mainly because most people spend insufficient time outdoors.","Concerns about sunscreen ingredients are more scientifically established than the risks associated with sunlight."]', 'B', NULL, ('2026-09-01 09:49:42'::timestamp AT TIME ZONE 'UTC'), NULL),
  (444, 6, 'LBING', 19, 'IS', 'C3', 'In 2000, a neuroscientist at University College London named Eleanor Maguire wanted to find out what effect, if any, all that driving around the labyrinthine streets of London might have on cabbies’ brains. When she brought sixteen taxi drivers into her lab and examined their brains in an MRI scanner, she found one surprising and important difference. The right posterior hippocampus, a part of the brain ...(19. know)... to be involved in spatial navigation, was 7 percent larger than normal in the cabbies—a small but very significant difference. Maguire concluded that all of that way-finding around London had physically altered the gross structure of their brains. The more years a cabbie had been on the road, the more pronounced the effect.', 'the word in the bracket could be..…', NULL, '[]', 'Known', NULL, ('2026-09-01 09:49:42'::timestamp AT TIME ZONE 'UTC'), NULL),
  (445, 6, 'LBING', 20, 'IS', 'C3', 'In 2000, a neuroscientist at University College London named Eleanor Maguire wanted to find out what effect, if any, all that driving around the labyrinthine streets of London might have on cabbies’ brains. When she brought sixteen taxi drivers into her lab and examined their brains in an MRI scanner, she found one surprising and important difference. The right posterior hippocampus, a part of the brain ...(19. know)... to be involved in spatial navigation, was 7 percent larger than normal in the cabbies—a small but very significant difference. Maguire concluded that all of that way-finding around London had physically altered the gross structure of their brains. The more years a cabbie had been on the road, the more pronounced the effect.', 'What part of the brain was found to be larger in London taxi drivers as a result of their extensive spatial navigation experience?', NULL, '[]', 'hippocampus', NULL, ('2026-09-01 09:49:42'::timestamp AT TIME ZONE 'UTC'), NULL),
  (446, 6, 'PPU', 1, 'PG', 'C3', '(1) Nelayan Kepulauan Riau (Kepri) mengeluhkan kebijakan penangkapan ikan terukur (PIT), terutama yang berkaitan dengan aturan zona tangkap nelayan. (2) Bahkan, belakangan ini, Kementerian Kelautan dan Perikanan (KKP) mewajibkan kapal yang beroperasi di atas 12 mil untuk memasang peralatan canggih, yaitu vessel monitoring system (VMS) atau alat pemantau kapal. (3) Selain itu, baru-baru ini, 3 kapal asal Kabupaten Bintan dan 2 kapal asal Tanjungpinang, Kepri, tertangkap kapal patroli Pengawasan Sumber Daya Kelautan dan Perikanan (PSDKP) Batam. (4) Kelima kapal tersebut melanggar aturan zona tangkap yang tertuang dalam Peraturan Menteri Kelautan dan Perikanan RI Nomor 59/Permen-KP/2020 tentang Jalur Penangkapan Ikan (JPI) dan Alat Penangkapan Ikan (API). (5) Kapal tersebut tertangkap walaupun menggunakan API jaring insang hanyut dan sudah mengantongi izin Pemerintah Kepri. (6) Hal tersebut menjelaskan bahwa kapal hanya boleh beroperasi di bawah 12 mil. (7) Namun, saat itu, kapal beroperasi di atas 12 mil atau di titik 17 mil sedang. (8) Selain itu, kapal yang menggunakan jaring hela dasar harus mempunyai izin dari pemerintah daerah. (9) Oleh sebab itu, banyak nelayan merasa pusing dengan kebijakan tersebut karena banyak nelayan yang melaut mengikuti musim sehingga tidak dapat dibatasi oleh garis imajiner tersebut.', 'Sesuai dengan konteks dalam teks tersebut, kata canggih (kalimat 2) memiliki makna yang berlawanan dengan kata…', NULL, '["kompleks.","maju.","ruwet.","modern.","kuno."]', 'E', NULL, ('2026-09-01 10:09:47'::timestamp AT TIME ZONE 'UTC'), NULL),
  (447, 6, 'PPU', 2, 'PG', 'C3', '(1) Nelayan Kepulauan Riau (Kepri) mengeluhkan kebijakan penangkapan ikan terukur (PIT), terutama yang berkaitan dengan aturan zona tangkap nelayan. (2) Bahkan, belakangan ini, Kementerian Kelautan dan Perikanan (KKP) mewajibkan kapal yang beroperasi di atas 12 mil untuk memasang peralatan canggih, yaitu vessel monitoring system (VMS) atau alat pemantau kapal. (3) Selain itu, baru-baru ini, 3 kapal asal Kabupaten Bintan dan 2 kapal asal Tanjungpinang, Kepri, tertangkap kapal patroli Pengawasan Sumber Daya Kelautan dan Perikanan (PSDKP) Batam. (4) Kelima kapal tersebut melanggar aturan zona tangkap yang tertuang dalam Peraturan Menteri Kelautan dan Perikanan RI Nomor 59/Permen-KP/2020 tentang Jalur Penangkapan Ikan (JPI) dan Alat Penangkapan Ikan (API). (5) Kapal tersebut tertangkap walaupun menggunakan API jaring insang hanyut dan sudah mengantongi izin Pemerintah Kepri. (6) Hal tersebut menjelaskan bahwa kapal hanya boleh beroperasi di bawah 12 mil. (7) Namun, saat itu, kapal beroperasi di atas 12 mil atau di titik 17 mil sedang. (8) Selain itu, kapal yang menggunakan jaring hela dasar harus mempunyai izin dari pemerintah daerah. (9) Oleh sebab itu, banyak nelayan merasa pusing dengan kebijakan tersebut karena banyak nelayan yang melaut mengikuti musim sehingga tidak dapat dibatasi oleh garis imajiner tersebut.', 'Ungkapan kapal beroperasi di atas 12 mil atau di titik 17 mil sedang dalam kalimat (7) akan sempurna apabila diperbaiki menjadi…', NULL, '["sedang kapal beroperasi di atas 12 mil atau di titik 17 mil.","beroperasi kapal atas di sedang 12 mil atau di titik 17 mil.","kapal sedang beroperasi di atas 12 mil atau di titik 17 mil.","kapal beroperasi atas di sedang 12 mil atau di titik 17 mil.","di kapal sedang beroperasi di atas 12 mil atau titik 17 mil."]', 'C', NULL, ('2026-09-01 10:09:47'::timestamp AT TIME ZONE 'UTC'), NULL),
